## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of persistent data structures, real-world versioning systems, and memory-time trade-offs. It also probes understanding of shared nodes and functional update semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Fully persistent data structures via path-copying
- Implicit treap or segment tree with augmented sums and sizes
- Version management with O(log n) mutations per update
- Correct boundary handling for empty intervals and edge operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(val, prio, left = null, right = null) {
    this.val = val;
    this.prio = prio;
    this.left = left;
    this.right = right;
    this.size = 1;
    this.sum = val;
  }

  update() {
    this.size = 1 + (this.left?.size ?? 0) + (this.right?.size ?? 0);
    this.sum = this.val + (this.left?.sum ?? 0) + (this.right?.sum ?? 0);
  }
}

function split(root, k) {
  if (!root) return [null, null];
  const leftSize = root.left?.size ?? 0;
  if (k <= leftSize) {
    const [l, r] = split(root.left, k);
    root.left = r;
    root.update();
    return [l, root];
  } else {
    const [l, r] = split(root.right, k - leftSize - 1);
    root.right = l;
    root.update();
    return [root, r];
  }
}

function merge(left, right) {
  if (!left || !right) return left ?? right;
  if (left.prio < right.prio) {
    left.right = merge(left.right, right);
    left.update();
    return left;
  } else {
    right.left = merge(left, right.left);
    right.update();
    return right;
  }
}

function rangeSum(root, l, r) {
  const [, mid] = split(root, l);
  const [target, rest] = split(mid, r - l + 1);
  const result = target?.sum ?? 0;
  return merge(merge(null, target), rest);
}

function insertAt(root, index, val) {
  const [left, right] = split(root, index);
  const newNode = new Node(val, Math.random());
  return merge(merge(left, newNode), right);
}

function eraseAt(root, index) {
  const [left, mid] = split(root, index);
  const [, right] = split(mid, 1);
  return merge(left, right);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Dynamic connectivity with insertions and deletions is a classic hard problem. This offline approach tests understanding of edge lifetimes and versioning, and how to combine segment trees with DSU rollback for log-scale updates.

## Key Concepts
- DSU with rollback (no path compression) to enable undo.
- Segment tree over time to map edge active intervals to time ranges.
- Offline processing: traverse tree, apply unions, answer leaf queries, then rollback.

## Code Example
```javascript
class RollbackDSU {
  constructor(n){
    this.parent = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i);
    this.sz = Array(n).fill(1);
    this.ch = [];
  }
  find(x){ while (this.parent[x]!==x) x = this.parent[x]; return x; }
  union(a,b){
    a = this.find(a); b = this.find(b);
    if (a===b){ this.ch.push(null); return; }
    if (this.sz[a] < this.sz[b]) [a,b] = [b,a];
    this.ch.push([b, this.parent[b], a, this.sz[a]]);
    this.parent[b] = a; this.sz[a] += this.sz[b];
  }
  snapshot(){ return this.ch.length; }
  rollback(snap){
    while (this.ch.length > snap){
      const ch = this.ch.pop();
      if (!ch) continue;
      const [b, oldP, a, oldS] = ch;
      this.parent[b] = oldP; this.sz[a] = oldS;
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Candidates must handle time-decayed scoring with dynamic insert/erase and frequent top-k queries over a moving window, a realistic pattern in recommender and CDN analytics at large scale.

## Key Concepts

- Time-decayed scoring and exponential decay
- Windowed top-k queries with lazy deletions
- Maintaining running sums in a sliding window
- Trade-offs: lazy vs eager pruning, memory vs accuracy

## Code Example

```javascript
class TimeDecayStore {
  constructor(lambda) { 
    this.lambda = lambda; 
    this.map = new Map(); 
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); 
    this.currentT = 0; 
  }

  insert(id, score, t) {
    this.map.set(id, { score, t_last: t });
    this.heap.insert({ id, decayedScore: score, t });
  }

  update(id, score, t) {
    this.map.set(id, { score, t_last: t });
    this.heap.insert({ id, decayedScore: score, t });
  }

  erase(id) {
    this.map.delete(id);
  }

  topKInLastWindow(k, t, W) {
    this.currentT = t;
    this.pruneExpired(t - W);
    const results = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (results.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const item = this.heap.extractMax();
      const current = this.map.get(item.id);

      if (current) {
        const decayed = current.score * Math.exp(-this.lambda * (t - current.t_last));
        if (current.t_last >= t - W) {
          results.push({ id: item.id, decayedScore: decayed });
          temp.push({ id: item.id, decayedScore: decayed, t: current.t_last });
        }
      }
    }

    temp.forEach(item => this.heap.insert(item));
    return results;
  }

  sumScoresInWindow(t, W) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (const [id, { score, t_last }] of this.map) {
      if (t_last >= t - W) {
        sum += score * Math.exp(-this.lambda * (t - t_last));
      }
    }
    return sum;
  }

  pruneExpired(windowStart) {
    // Lazy pruning during queries
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a scalable data structure for tracking peak occupancy across a large domain with many overlapping intervals—a common problem in campaign management, resource allocation, and scheduling systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Implicit segment tree**: Enables efficient operations over a massive coordinate space (up to 2^31-1) without building a full tree
- **Range add, range max with argmax**: Combines lazy propagation for updates with maintaining both value and position of the maximum
- **Incremental updates**: Insert and erase operations dynamically modify the tree state
- **Handling duplicates**: Multiple intervals covering the same coordinate naturally accumulate via additive weights

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.max = 0;
    this.argMax = 0;
    this.lazy = 0;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}

function rangeAdd(node, L, R, ql, qr, delta) {
  if (qr < L || R < ql) return;
  if (ql <= L && R <= qr) {
    node.max += delta;
    node.lazy += delta;
    return;
  }
  push(node);
  const mid = (L + R) >>> 1;
  rangeAdd(node.left, L, mid, ql, qr, delta);
  rangeAdd(node.right, mid + 1, R, ql, qr, delta);
  pull(node);
}
```

